+## Phase 2 (spec/requests): plan, what changed, results
+
+### Plan items and status
+
+1. Profile `spec/requests` the same way. **Done.** Hot spots differed from
+   models: webhook serialization, route reloads and per-example setup in one
+   file, not SQL or HTTP.
+2. `cache_spec.rb` (258 examples, the largest file). **Done**, see change 3.
+3. Shared examples in `spec/support/examples/api.rb` (239 examples). **Checked,
+   not changed.** About 35 ms each. The JSON attributes the time of the group
+   that includes them to `api.rb`: each example builds a user (about 10 ms) and
+   a token, then makes one request. What remains is factory cost and the `before`
+   blocks of the including groups (see "Next candidates").
+4. Extend the webhook skip to `type: :request`. **Done**, see change 1. Checked
+   first: `spec/requests/admin/webhooks_spec.rb` only posts an invalid webhook,
+   and no request spec asserts on webhook delivery or payloads. The skip only
+   applies when no enabled webhook subscribes to the event, so it cannot change
+   what is enqueued.
+5. Global state assigned at load time. **Checked: none** in `spec/requests`
+   (`grep -rnE "^\s{0,2}[A-Za-z:]+\.[a-z_]+\s*=[^=~]"` is empty). No `sleep`, no
+   `use_transactional_tests: false`, and no `before(:all)` before this phase.
+
+### Findings (CPU sample shares before this phase, requests)
+
+- Webhook serialization: `WebhookService#call` was about 22% of CPU
+  (`TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` 23%, `serialize_event` 20%,
+  `SerializableResource#to_json` 22%). About 6.7k subscriber lookups and tens of
+  thousands of serializer queries per run (for example `user_ips` and
+  `user_invite_requests` at about 5k each, `account_stats` at about 7.6k). After:
+  about 1.5%.
+- `reload_routes!`: about 4% of CPU. `signature_verification_spec.rb` and
+  `api/v1/csp_spec.rb` ran a full route reload after every example: 31 reloads of
+  about 0.17 s each. After: about 0.3%.
+- `cache_spec.rb`: each of its 258 examples rebuilt the same 8 records (alice, a
+  moderator, two statuses, an invite, a poll, a token, a follow). That was about
+  50 ms per example, about 70% of the file.
+- Time split before this phase (unprofiled hook run, 136 s of example time):
+  about 24 s in SQL (about 18%), about 22 s in request dispatch (1956 requests,
+  about 11 ms each), and the rest in Ruby (fixtures, callbacks, specs).
+
+### What changed
+
+1. `spec/support/skip_unsubscribed_webhook_serialization.rb`: the skip is enabled
+   for `type: :request` as well as `type: :model` (`TYPES` constant). The around
+   hook now runs for every example and restores the previous value.
+2. `spec/requests/signature_verification_spec.rb` and
+   `spec/requests/api/v1/csp_spec.rb`: `after { reload_routes! }` became
+   `after(:all) { reload_routes! }`. Each example still draws its own test routes
+   in `before`, so the examples see the same routes. The app routes are restored
+   once per group. Both `after(:all)` lines carry an inline
+   `rubocop:disable RSpec/BeforeAfterAll`, as in the rest of the spec suite.
+3. `spec/requests/cache_spec.rb`: the shared records are created in
+   `before(:context)` instead of `before`. They live in a connection pinned for the
+   group (`ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.pin_connection!(true)`, which begins
+   a non-joinable transaction). `after(:context)` unpins it and rolls back.
+   Each example still runs in its own transactional fixture, nested inside the
+   pinned one, so database changes made by examples are rolled back as before.
+   `Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` is set in the group hook, because rails_helper
+   sets it per example and that has not run yet at `before(:context)`.
+   Known side effects, checked:
+   - Redis data written by the setup (timelines and similar feeds) is removed by
+     the global `after` hook after the first example. No assertion in
+     `cache_spec.rb` reads timelines or response bodies.
+   - Verified: 258 examples, 0 failures with seeds 1, 4242 and 98765. The test
+     database has no leftover rows after the run, and no open transaction or
+     pinned connection remains.
+
+Pattern for later use: shared records that examples only read can be created
+once per group this way. Before doing it in a file, check that the examples do
+not depend on Redis-held data, and that the records are not defined in a `let`
+that differs per context.
+
+### Results (requests, seed 4242, example time from the JSON `duration`)
+
+| Step | Duration | Change vs previous | Cumulative vs pristine |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| Pristine (HEAD), single run | 163.8 s | | |
+| Phase 1 state (log level only), single run | 140.5 s | -14% | -14% |
+| 1+2. Webhook skip for requests, routes reloaded once per group, single run | 95.3 s | -32% | -42% |
+| 3. Shared records in `cache_spec.rb`, single run | 79.7 s | -16% | -51% |
+| Final, paired with pristine (seed 4242), pristine | 154.3 s | | |
+| Final, paired with pristine (seed 4242), current | 81.2 s | | -47% |
+
+The single-run ladder carries the noise described above. The paired final
+number is the reference.
+
+Per file, paired (pristine to current, seconds):
+
+| spec file | before | after | change |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| `requests/cache_spec.rb` | 32.6 | 3.3 | -90% |
+| `requests/signature_verification_spec.rb` | 6.2 | 0.5 | -92% |
+| `requests/activitypub/replies_spec.rb` | 14.1 | 8.1 | -43% |
+| `support/examples/api.rb` (shared examples) | 13.1 | 8.7 | -33% |
+| `requests/api/v2/notifications_spec.rb` | 7.1 | 4.9 | -32% |
+| `requests/api/v1/notifications_spec.rb` | 4.7 | 3.9 | -16% |
+| `requests/activitypub/contexts_spec.rb` | 3.5 | 1.8 | -48% |
+| `requests/activitypub/outboxes_spec.rb` | 2.5 | 1.2 | -53% |
+| `requests/accounts_spec.rb` | 2.1 | 1.3 | -40% |
+| **total (sum of example time)** | **154.3** | **81.2** | **-47%** |
+
+Combined: `spec/models` 49.7 s (1795 examples, 0 failures, same as after phase 1)
+plus `spec/requests` 81.2 s is about 131 s. The baseline is about 283 s (paired
+pristine, using the phase 1 paired models mean of about 128 s) or 299 s (single
+runs). That is about 2.15x to 2.3x, so the 2x target is met.
+
+SQL per requests run: 141,960 queries, down from 234,378 at the phase 1 state
+(-39%). Most of the drop comes from the webhook serializers and the per-example
+fixtures in `cache_spec.rb`. The remaining largest templates are savepoint and release
+(about 22.9k together), `user_roles` by id (about 9.0k), `settings` reads (about
+7.4k) and `username_blocks` checks (about 7.2k).
+
+### Profile after phase 2 (requests, CPU sample shares, inclusive unless noted)
+
+- `Fabricate` (fixtures): about 57%. This is the biggest remaining cost and
+  belongs to the factories phase.
+- BCrypt: about 5.8% (self). Each fabricated user hashes its password at bcrypt's
+  minimum cost (about 1.1 ms). Factories phase.
+- GC: about 6% (sweeping and marking).
+- Mail: `ActionMailer::MailDeliveryJob` about 5.5%, of which Premailer inline CSS
+  about 4.3%. Mail comes from app actions (notifications, reports, moderation),
+  not from fixtures. No request spec asserts on mail content. A possible later
+  change, but it touches mail rendering for all specs.
+- `Setting` reads about 1.8% and `UserRole.everyone` about 1.5%: both app code.
+  `Rails.cache.clear` after each example stays. Without it, rolled-back settings
+  rows would leave stale cache values.
+- `reload_routes`: about 0.3% (was about 4%).
+- `WebhookService#call`: about 1.5% (was about 22%).
+
+### Next candidates (not done; each needs a look at the examples)
+
+- `spec/requests/api/v1/notifications_spec.rb` and
+  `spec/requests/api/v2/notifications_spec.rb`: `before` blocks call
+  `PostStatusService`, `ReblogService`, `FavouriteService` and friends for each
+  example, at 250-400 ms per example. Sharing them needs the `let(:user)` and
+  `let(:bob)` records to become lookups, and the group fixtures to be built from
+  fixed values. Grouped notifications depend on timestamps. Estimated gain about
+  5 s, with more risk than `cache_spec.rb`.
+- `spec/requests/activitypub/replies_spec.rb`: 45 examples at about 180 ms each.
+  `let(:status)` varies by context (visibility), and `before` blocks change
+  accounts (suspensions, deletions). Not a simple share.
+- Factories phase (next): `Fabricate(:status)` about 20 ms, `Fabricate(:user)`
+  about 10 ms (account, two login activities, bcrypt). These drive the 57%.
+
+### Tried and rejected in this phase
+
+- StackProf wall mode with raw samples: 966 MB dump, out of memory when loaded.
+  Use CPU mode and aggregated output (see "Analysis snippets").
+- Skipping all serialization in request specs (not only when no enabled
+  subscriber exists): not done. The subscriber check keeps the behaviour the same
+  wherever a webhook exists (`admin/webhooks_spec.rb`).
 
